        <div id="why-tiles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Consider Garage Floor Tiles Over Epoxy or Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Garage floor epoxy</strong> and <strong>garage floor coatings</strong> get most of the attention in home improvement content, and for good reason — a properly applied epoxy system creates a seamless, chemical-resistant surface that looks fantastic. But epoxy has real practical downsides that tiles simply don't share. A DIY epoxy job requires grinding or acid-etching the concrete, mixing precise two-part chemical ratios, working against a limited pot life before the material starts curing in the bucket, and then keeping the garage completely empty for 24 to 72 hours while it cures — longer before it reaches full chemical resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Interlocking garage floor tiles</strong> skip nearly all of that. Most rigid PVC and rubber tile systems snap together by hand with no adhesive, no mixing, and no cure time — you can drive a car onto a freshly installed tile floor the same day. They also solve a maintenance problem epoxy can't: if a section gets damaged by a dropped engine block or years of jack-stand wear, you replace that one tile instead of grinding out and repatching an entire bonded coating. And because most tile systems sit on top of the slab rather than bonding to it, they don't require the crack repair, moisture testing, and surface prep that a failed or cracked concrete slab demands before epoxy can be applied.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Tiles vs. Epoxy: The Time Difference</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4-8 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical DIY tile install, 2-car garage</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2-4 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical DIY epoxy job incl. cure time</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Same Day</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tiles: park a car after install</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">1 tile</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">What you replace when tiles get damaged, vs. re-coating the whole floor</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That said, tiles aren't strictly better in every way. A well-installed epoxy floor has no seams at all, which matters in a garage where you're sweeping up fine sawdust or metal shavings that can work into tile joints over time. Epoxy is also usually the cheaper option per square foot for a professionally installed floor. We cover the full trade-off in detail in the <a href="#tiles-vs-epoxy">tiles vs. epoxy comparison</a> further down this guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Tiles -->
        <div id="types-of-tiles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Garage Floor Tiles: Which One Is Right for Your Space?
          </h2>

          <p>
            "Garage floor tiles" covers several genuinely different products, and picking the wrong category is the most common mistake we see — a peel-and-stick vinyl tile and a rigid interlocking PVC tile solve different problems and shouldn't be chosen on price alone.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rigid PVC Interlocking Tiles</h3>
          <p>
            The most common and most durable category, used by brands like <strong>SWISSTRAX</strong>, <strong>RaceDeck</strong>, <strong>IncStores</strong>, and <strong>TrafficMaster</strong>. These snap together via a loop-and-peg edge system with no adhesive, sit slightly raised off the slab for drainage, and are rated for vehicle weight, jack stands, and rolling tool chests. They're the right default choice for most garages, workshops, and daily-driver parking.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rubber Interlocking Tiles</h3>
          <p>
            Softer and heavier than PVC, rubber tiles like <strong>Rubber-Cal Coin-Grip</strong> trade some rigidity for genuinely superior impact absorption and sound dampening — a dropped tool lands with a dull thud instead of a sharp clang, and standing on them for hours is noticeably easier on your legs. They're the strongest choice for a garage doubling as a home gym or a workshop where you're on your feet all day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Peel-and-Stick Vinyl Tiles</h3>
          <p>
            Products like <strong>G-Floor RaceDay</strong> adhere directly to the concrete rather than interlocking with each other. They're thinner, cheaper, and dramatically faster to install than rigid tile systems, but the trade-off is permanence — properly bonded adhesive tiles aren't designed to be pulled up and relaid elsewhere, and they demand more thorough surface prep since the adhesive needs to bond to clean, dry, crack-free concrete.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garage Carpet and Roll-Out Mats</h3>
          <p>
            A separate category entirely from hard tile: garage carpet tiles and large roll-out mats offer a softer, warmer surface, often at the lowest price point, but with meaningfully less chemical and impact resistance than PVC or rubber tile. They suit a garage used more for storage, hobbies, or overflow living space than active vehicle and tool work, where oil resistance and jack-stand-rated durability matter less.
          </p>

