        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Install Garage Floor Tiles: The Complete DIY Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Installing interlocking garage floor tiles is one of the most approachable flooring projects a DIYer can take on — no special tools or chemical handling required. Here's the process we followed for every rigid and rubber tile in this test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Sweep and Measure</h3>
          <p>
            Sweep the slab thoroughly and remove any loose debris — unlike epoxy, you don't need to grind or acid-etch the concrete, since tiles simply sit on top of it. Measure the full garage floor, including any storage nooks or extensions, and order 5-10% extra tile to account for border cuts and mistakes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Plan Your Layout</h3>
          <p>
            Dry-fit a row of tiles across the widest point of the garage before committing to a starting position, so border cuts end up roughly even on both sides rather than a full tile on one wall and a sliver on the other. If you're planning a two-tone or checkerboard pattern, map it out on paper first — reworking a pattern mid-installation wastes tiles and time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Start From a Back Corner and Work Outward</h3>
          <p>
            Begin in the corner farthest from the garage door and work toward the opening, snapping each tile's loop-and-peg edges into the adjacent tile by hand. Use a rubber mallet to fully seat any joints that don't click together cleanly on the first try — a gap that isn't fully seated will work itself loose under vehicle weight over time.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Cut Border Tiles to Fit</h3>
          <p>
            Once the full-size tiles are down, measure and cut border pieces to fit around walls, support posts, and door tracks. Rigid PVC tiles typically cut cleanly with a jigsaw; rubber tiles can usually be trimmed with a sharp utility knife. Leave a small gap — generally 1/4 to 1/2 inch depending on the manufacturer — between the tile field and the walls to allow for thermal expansion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Check the Garage Door Clearance</h3>
          <p>
            Rigid PVC and rubber tiles add measurable height to the floor — typically 3/8 to 3/4 inch depending on the system — which can interfere with the garage door's bottom seal or a car's ground clearance on a ramp-style threshold. Test the door through a full cycle before considering the job finished, and trim the door's bottom seal if needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Tiles vs Epoxy vs Paint vs Carpet -->
        <div id="tiles-vs-epoxy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Garage Floor Tiles vs. Epoxy vs. Paint vs. Carpet: Which Should You Choose?
          </h2>

          <p>
            All four options solve the same underlying problem — bare concrete is porous, stains easily, and looks unfinished — but each makes a different trade-off between cost, durability, installation effort, and finished look.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Tiles If...</h3>
          <p>
            You want a fast DIY install with no cure time, you want the option to replace individual damaged sections instead of re-coating the whole floor, or your slab has cracks or an old failing coating you don't want to deal with before flooring goes down. Tiles are also the right call if you might ever want to pull the flooring up and take it with you, since most rigid systems are fully removable and reusable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Epoxy If...</h3>
          <p>
            You want a completely seamless surface with no joints for fine debris to collect in, you're comfortable with (or hiring out) a multi-day install including cure time, and you want the lowest cost per square foot for a permanent, professional-grade finish. See our full <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">Best Garage Floor Epoxy</a> roundup and Garage Floor Paint vs. Epoxy comparison for the complete breakdown.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Paint If...</h3>
          <p>
            Budget is the top priority and you're willing to accept a shorter lifespan and more frequent touch-ups than epoxy or tile. Concrete floor paint is the cheapest option per square foot and the easiest to apply, but it wears faster under vehicle traffic and offers less chemical resistance than either epoxy or a quality tile system.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Choose Carpet or Roll-Out Mats If...</h3>
          <p>
            The garage functions more as extra living, storage, or hobby space than active vehicle and tool work, and comfort or warmth underfoot matters more than chemical and impact resistance. Garage carpet is generally the fastest and cheapest option to install, but it's the least durable choice here for anything involving regular oil, gas, or heavy point loads.
          </p>
        </div>

