          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are garage floor tiles better than epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on priorities. Garage floor tiles install faster (a weekend versus multiple days including cure time), can be walked and driven on immediately, and let you replace a single damaged tile instead of patching an entire coating. Epoxy creates a seamless, fully bonded surface with no seams for debris or moisture to collect in, and it's typically cheaper per square foot for a professional installation. For a DIY project on a timeline, or a garage where you want the option to lift and relay flooring later, tiles usually win. For the most seamless, permanent, showroom-grade finish, professionally applied epoxy has the edge.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much do garage floor tiles cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget interlocking PVC tiles like TrafficMaster or IncStores Nitro typically run $3-5 per square foot. Premium rigid PVC tiles like SWISSTRAX Ribtrax Pro or RaceDeck Diamond run $5-8+ per square foot. Rubber interlocking tiles like Rubber-Cal Coin-Grip generally cost more than PVC due to material weight and shipping. Peel-and-stick vinyl tiles like G-Floor RaceDay are usually the cheapest per square foot among finished-look options. A standard two-car garage (roughly 400-500 sq ft) typically runs $1,500-4,000 in materials depending on tile tier, before any professional installation labor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put garage floor tiles over cracked concrete or old epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and this is one of the biggest practical advantages of interlocking tile systems over epoxy or paint. Rigid PVC and rubber interlocking tiles sit on top of the slab without bonding to it, so minor cracks, stains, or an old, peeling epoxy coating underneath generally don't need to be repaired first — the tiles simply bridge over them. Peel-and-stick vinyl tiles are the exception: because they rely on adhesive bonding directly to the concrete, the surface underneath needs to be clean, dry, and free of active cracking or flaking coatings for the adhesive to hold long-term.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do garage floor tiles trap moisture underneath?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Interlocking tiles are raised slightly off the slab and most designs include small drainage channels on the underside specifically to let moisture, meltwater, and vapor escape rather than pool underneath. In practice this means occasional slush or a minor water intrusion generally isn't a problem. However, a garage with a chronic moisture or flooding issue should address that underlying problem first — no flooring product, tile or otherwise, is a substitute for fixing a slab that regularly takes on standing water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can garage floor tiles support a car or heavy tool chest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Rigid PVC tiles like SWISSTRAX Ribtrax Pro, RaceDeck Diamond, and IncStores Nitro are specifically engineered to support the weight of passenger vehicles, rolling tool chests, and jack stands without cracking or permanent denting. Rubber tiles like Rubber-Cal Coin-Grip also handle vehicle weight well, with the added benefit of cushioning impact from dropped tools. Thinner, budget-tier PVC tiles handle typical vehicle weight adequately but are more prone to flexing or denting under concentrated point loads like a jack stand or a heavy engine hoist.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you install interlocking garage floor tiles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start in a back corner of the garage and work outward, snapping each tile's loop-and-peg edges into the adjacent tile by hand, using a rubber mallet to fully seat stubborn joints. Most systems require no adhesive, though a few installers use double-sided tape at the perimeter for extra hold in high-traffic areas. Cut border tiles to fit around walls and obstructions with a jigsaw or utility knife, depending on the material. A clean, swept, reasonably level slab is the only real surface prep required, and a two-car garage typically takes a single DIYer 4-8 hours to complete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What's the difference between garage floor tiles and garage carpet or roll-out mats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Garage carpet and roll-out mats are soft, fabric- or foam-backed coverings, typically installed as a single large piece rather than individual interlocking tiles. They're generally cheaper and faster to install than tile systems, and some are designed to be removable for cleaning, but they offer far less resistance to oil, gas, and chemical stains, and most aren't rated for the same heavy point loads — like jack stands or engine hoists — as rigid PVC or rubber tile. Roll-out mats work well for a garage used mainly as extra living or storage space; tiles are the better choice for active vehicle and workshop use.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

