        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Garage Floor Tile Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Interlocking tiles are the forgiving option for a bad slab, which is exactly why people skip the checks that still matter.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying Tiles Over an Uneven Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tiles follow the floor rather than levelling it. Over a slab with humps, dips, or a pronounced slope, the interlocks work loose and the tiles rock underfoot. Check with a long straightedge first, and grind high spots or fill low ones before laying anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Tiles Solve a Moisture Problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tiles sit above the slab rather than bonding to it, so vapour still comes up — it just ends up trapped underneath, where it grows mould and corrodes anything metal. On a slab with known moisture, choose an open-grid or vented tile that lets the floor breathe.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Thermal Expansion</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                PVC and polypropylene tiles expand and contract meaningfully across a garage-sized temperature range. Laid tight to all four walls in winter, they buckle in the middle in July. Leave the perimeter gap the manufacturer specifies and finish it with edge trim.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Tiles Too Thin for Vehicle Weight</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tiles rated for foot traffic and workshop use crack and deform under a jack stand or a heavy vehicle. Check the point-load rating, not just the thickness, and choose a solid rather than a coin-top grid tile anywhere a jack will land.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The <strong class="text-white">best garage floor tile</strong> for most people is <strong class="text-white">SWISSTRAX Ribtrax Pro</strong> — it delivers the strongest impact and chemical resistance of anything we tested, in a finish that hides everyday dirt and wear better than a showroom-glossy alternative. If a polished, car-show look matters more than day-to-day low maintenance, <strong class="text-white">RaceDeck Diamond</strong> is the better pick. On a budget, <strong class="text-white">IncStores Nitro</strong> gets the fundamentals right for meaningfully less money, and if your garage is unheated and sees hard winters, <strong class="text-white">DiamondTrax Home</strong> is built specifically not to crack on you.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Whichever system you choose, remember the core advantage tiles have over epoxy: <strong class="text-white">speed and repairability</strong>. A weekend installs the whole floor, you can park a car on it the same day, and if one section ever gets damaged, you replace that single tile instead of redoing the entire floor.
